<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!--
  Suppressions for the OWASP dependency-check scan (plan task 106).

  Every entry here is a claim that a finding does not apply, and each one states the evidence for
  that claim. A suppression without a reason is indistinguishable from hiding a vulnerability, so
  none is added without one, and none is open-ended: each is pinned to a specific CVE against a
  specific artifact, so a *different* CVE against the same jar still fails the build.

  Re-check these whenever a dependency's major version moves — the version-range arguments below
  stop holding the moment the version changes.
-->
<suppressions xmlns="https://jeremylong.github.io/DependencyCheck/dependency-suppression.1.3.xsd">

    <!--
      dependency-check reads Spring Boot's `spring-boot-mongodb` / `spring-boot-data-mongodb`
      modules (version 4.1.0, matching the Boot release train) and generates the CPE
      `cpe:2.3:a:mongodb:mongodb:4.1.0` — i.e. it concludes these Java libraries *are* MongoDB
      Server 4.1.0. They are not; they are Spring's auto-configuration modules. The three CVEs
      below are all MongoDB Server defects (mongod-side aggregation and command handling) with no
      code path in a client library at all.

      Note for whoever reads this next: these being false positives *for the Java dependency* says
      nothing about the MongoDB server this application talks to. That runs as a container image
      and is out of this scan's scope entirely — see GO-LIVE-CHECKLIST.md, which tracks keeping the
      mongo image patched separately.
    -->
    <suppress>
        <notes>MongoDB Server CVE matched to a Spring Boot module via a spurious mongodb:mongodb CPE.</notes>
        <packageUrl regex="true">^pkg:maven/org\.springframework\.boot/spring-boot-(starter-|data-)?mongodb@.*$</packageUrl>
        <cve>CVE-2021-32036</cve>
    </suppress>
    <suppress>
        <notes>MongoDB Server CVE (CISA KEV, server-side) matched to a Spring Boot module via a spurious CPE.</notes>
        <packageUrl regex="true">^pkg:maven/org\.springframework\.boot/spring-boot-(starter-|data-)?mongodb@.*$</packageUrl>
        <cve>CVE-2025-14847</cve>
    </suppress>
    <suppress>
        <notes>
            MongoDB Server CVE: $_internalApplyOplogUpdate is an aggregation stage executed by
            mongod. Matched to a Spring Boot module via a spurious CPE.
        </notes>
        <packageUrl regex="true">^pkg:maven/org\.springframework\.boot/spring-boot-(starter-|data-)?mongodb@.*$</packageUrl>
        <cve>CVE-2026-9753</cve>
    </suppress>

    <!--
      CVE-2026-41717 is a real Spring Data MongoDB vulnerability — SpEL injection through a @Query
      method using a capture-all placeholder — but it does not apply here, for two independent
      reasons:

        1. Affected versions are 5.0.0-5.0.5 and 4.5.0-4.5.x. This project resolves
           spring-data-mongodb 5.1.0, outside both ranges. The scanner matched it only because the
           spurious CPE above carries the Boot module version (4.1.0), not the real library version.
        2. The vulnerable construct is absent: there is not a single @Query annotation in this
           codebase. Repositories are derived-query or MongoTemplate-based throughout.

      The second reason is the durable one; the first stops holding if the version ever moves
      backwards.
    -->
    <suppress>
        <notes>Spring Data MongoDB SpEL injection via @Query: resolved version 5.1.0 is outside the affected range, and no @Query exists in this codebase.</notes>
        <packageUrl regex="true">^pkg:maven/org\.springframework\.boot/spring-boot-(starter-|data-)?mongodb@.*$</packageUrl>
        <cve>CVE-2026-41717</cve>
    </suppress>

    <!--
      Same shape of mistake, different product: `micrometer-registry-prometheus` is matched to
      `cpe:2.3:a:prometheus:prometheus`, as though the Micrometer client library were the Prometheus
      server. CVE-2026-42154 is a memory-exhaustion flaw in the server's remote-read endpoint
      (/api/v1/read) when decoding snappy-compressed bodies. This library has no such endpoint — it
      only formats metrics for a scraper to read.

      As above: the Prometheus *server* on the monitoring host is a separate thing, not covered by
      this scan, and does need to be on 3.5.3 / 3.11.3 or later.
    -->
    <suppress>
        <notes>Prometheus server remote-read CVE matched to the Micrometer client registry via a spurious prometheus:prometheus CPE.</notes>
        <packageUrl regex="true">^pkg:maven/io\.micrometer/micrometer-registry-prometheus@.*$</packageUrl>
        <cve>CVE-2026-42154</cve>
    </suppress>

</suppressions>
